            <ArticleTitle>Assessment, Treatment, and Prevention Strategies for Hair-Thread Tourniquet Syndrome in Infants.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>421-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.nwh.2016.06.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1751-4851(16)30154-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hair-thread tourniquets are a rare occurrence but result in significant injury as a hair or thread wraps around a digit, resulting in tissue swelling, pain, or possible tissue ischemia. This condition is often overlooked in the differential diagnosis for a fussy infant. Awareness of this condition will help nurses and other clinicians identify and treat the condition. Some simple prevention strategies can help parents and other caregivers mitigate risk.</AbstractText>
